Yes — **I agree with the direction of the theory, but not with every sentence of the assessment as written**. The important change is that our programme has now moved from *trying to discover the kernel directly* to **reconstructing the ontology and operation semantics that could justify a kernel**.

That is a substantial change.

## 1. What has changed fundamentally

Previously, the programme implicitly followed:

$$
\text{existing }K_t
\rightarrow
\text{operations }\mathcal O
\rightarrow
\text{transformation }\delta
\rightarrow
\text{kernel}
$$

The problem was that we were trying to define operations while several of their operands were themselves unresolved.

The revised direction is:

$$
\boxed{
\text{Reality}
\rightarrow O
\rightarrow \text{conceptual objects}
\rightarrow \text{relations}
\rightarrow \mathcal O
\rightarrow \delta
\rightarrow \text{operational kernel}
}
$$

So **Step 290 cannot simply mean "define the operations now."**

It must mean:

> **Determine what an operation means, what it acts upon, what it is allowed to change, what it must preserve, and under which semantic conditions it is defined — without prematurely selecting the final operation set.**

That distinction is critical.

