# 9. What the Gītā changes — and what it does not

The Gītā track now has a much healthier role.

It does **not** provide:

$$
\mathcal O
$$

It does not define:

$$
\delta
$$

It does not prove:

$$
Buddhi=\operatorname{Discriminate}
$$

Instead it gives conceptual lenses that may generate hypotheses.

For example:

$$
\text{Karma}
\leadsto
\text{hypothesis about action/result distinction}
$$

$$
\text{Buddhi}
\leadsto
\text{hypothesis about discrimination}
$$

$$
\text{Vairāgya}
\leadsto
\text{hypothesis about outcome independence}
$$

But the architecture must independently establish them.

That is exactly the principle discovered in 286:

$$
\boxed{
\text{corroboration}\neq\text{derivation}
}
$$

And Cavell adds another valuable mechanism:

$$
\boxed{
\text{philosophical objection}
\rightarrow
\text{candidate missing concept}
}
$$

rather than:

$$
\text{philosophical objection}
\rightarrow
\text{new primitive}
$$

